  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 PM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nTroughing continues to slide over the Great Lakes today and tonight \nbefore amplified ridging takes ahold of most of the CONUS east of \nthe Mississippi into this weekend. Subsidence aloft will keep \nsurface high pressure centered over the Mid-Atlantic, nosing into \nthe Great Lakes through Saturday. Troughing will eventually eject \nover the Great Plains and slide over the region on Sunday, \nsupporting a relatively weak, elongated cyclone that is expected to \ntrek into the Great Lakes Sunday into Monday morning. Surface high \npressure then looks to build into the region as we head into the \nmiddle of next week.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nSeasonably-mild temperatures will stick around the Northwoods \nthrough at least Saturday and likely Sunday with aforementioned \nridging in place. Highs well into the 50s and 60s are anticipated \nwith the warmest temperatures coming on Saturday. Record high \ntemperatures will be possible across some areas Saturday afternoon \nwith localized readings in the mid to upper 60s. These pleasant-for-\nmany temperatures will abruptly come to an end later Sunday, \nhowever, as the cold front swings across the Great Lakes and drops \nreadings into the 20s and 30s through the middle of next week.\n\nThis will also mark the return of precip chances across northern \nMichigan as rain arrives with the front -- most likely Sunday \nafternoon/evening. As noted by the previous forecaster, mainly light \nrain is expected due to a relative lack of moisture with most areas \nstruggling to see 0.25\" by Monday morning. Lake effect snow chances \nalso enter the picture Sunday night into Monday, but current \nconfidence is that any activity will mainly be flurries and \naccumulation will be difficult to come by. Some intensification of \nshowers may be possible later Monday/Monday night, but chances are \nlow at this time. Afterwards, precip-free weather is in store \nthrough the middle of the week as we head towards Thanksgiving.",
